# Thermal Detection — Logic

## Overview

This module handles **health monitoring** of the solar panel using two sensors:
- **MLX90614** — non-contact infrared thermometer (detects cell damage via hotspot)
- **LDR** — light dependent resistor (detects dirt/dust/dropping via reflected light)

These two sensors together answer two different questions:
- Is the panel **dirty**? → LDR
- Is the panel **damaged**? → MLX90614

---

## Sensor 1 — LDR (Dirt Detection)

### How it works

The LDR sits on the robot facing downward toward the panel surface.

Clean panel → reflects light strongly → LDR reads HIGH value  
Dirty panel → dirt absorbs/scatters light → LDR reads LOW value

### Why ratio and not raw value

Raw LDR values change with weather (sunny vs cloudy).  
To make detection weather-independent, we calculate:

```
ldrRatio = ldrRaw / 1023.0
```

This ratio stays consistent regardless of ambient lighting conditions.  
A drop in ratio means dirt — not a change in weather.

### Threshold

```
ldrRatio < 0.60  →  DIRTY — trigger cleaning
```

Threshold is set after physical calibration on a clean panel.

---

## Sensor 2 — MLX90614 (Cell Damage Detection)

### How it works

MLX90614 is a non-contact infrared thermometer.  
It reads panel surface temperature WITHOUT touching the panel.  
The robot moves across the panel and scans temperature at every point.

### Why not use raw temperature

Raw temperature changes with weather:
- Sunny day → panel at 55°C
- Cloudy day → panel at 28°C

A fixed threshold would cause false alarms on cloudy days.

### Solution — tempDiff (relative measurement)

Instead of raw temperature, we use:

```
tempDiff = objectTemp - ambientTemp
```

This is the difference between panel surface and surrounding air temperature.  
This difference stays meaningful regardless of weather conditions.

| Condition | objectTemp | ambientTemp | tempDiff |
|---|---|---|---|
| Sunny, normal cell | 55°C | 35°C | 20°C |
| Cloudy, normal cell | 28°C | 25°C | 3°C |
| Sunny, damaged cell | 73°C | 35°C | 38°C |
| Cloudy, damaged cell | 46°C | 25°C | 21°C |

A damaged cell always shows tempDiff **significantly higher** than neighbours — regardless of weather.

### Why NOT use cool spot detection

A cool spot (dropping blocking sunlight) causes low tempDiff.  
But on a cloudy day, the whole panel has low tempDiff naturally.  
This causes false alarms — cleaning triggered when panel is actually clean.  
Therefore, **cool spot detection via MLX is intentionally removed.**  
LDR handles dirt detection instead (reliable in all weather).

### Threshold

```
tempDiff > (neighbour average + 15°C)  →  HOTSPOT → cell damage alert
```

15°C above neighbours is the threshold — based on standard hotspot research.

---

## Decision Logic

```
Robot moving across panel →

Every 1 second:
    Read ldrRatio
    Read tempDiff (objectTemp - ambientTemp)

    IF ldrRatio < 0.60:
        → DIRT DETECTED
        → Stop robot
        → Trigger cleaning system
        → After cleaning, re-read LDR
        → If recovered → resume moving
        → If not recovered → send alert (stubborn dirt)

    ELSE IF tempDiff > (neighbour + 15°C):
        → HOTSPOT DETECTED (cell damage)
        → Stop robot
        → Log position
        → Send WiFi alert to owner
        → Resume moving (cleaning cannot fix damage)

    ELSE:
        → NORMAL
        → Keep moving
```

---

## Why these two sensors together

| Sensor | Detects | Method | Weather dependent? |
|---|---|---|---|
| LDR | Dirt / dust / dropping | Reflected light ratio drops | NO — ratio compensates |
| MLX90614 | Cell damage (hotspot) | tempDiff spikes vs neighbours | NO — relative measure |

LDR answers: **is the surface dirty?**  
MLX answers: **is the panel internally damaged?**  
Together they give complete panel health information.

---

## Calibration

Run `calibration` file first before using this sensing logic.  
Observe serial monitor output and set:
- `LDR_DIRTY_RATIO` — midpoint between clean and dirty LDR ratio readings
- `HOTSPOT_DIFF` — based on tempDiff observed during normal operation (add 15°C to that)

Recalibrate if panel location, season, or panel brand changes.
